## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to adapt sorting techniques to handle duplicate elements efficiently and to analyze complexity trade-offs between quicksort variants and mergesort.

## Key Concepts
- Three-way partition using Dutch national flag algorithm
- Average vs worst-case complexity with duplicate keys
- In-place sorting with tail recursion optimization
- Space complexity comparison between quicksort and mergesort

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton: threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, hi) with lt/gt pointers
function threeWayQuicksort(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  if (lo >= hi) return;

  const pivot = a[lo];
  let lt = lo, i = lo + 1, gt = hi;

  while (i <= gt) {
    if (a[i] < pivot) swap(a, lt++, i++);
    else if (a[i] > pivot) swap(a, i, gt--);
    else i++;
  }

  // Tail recurse on smaller subarray for O(log n) space
  if (gt - lo < hi - lt) {
    threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, lt - 1);
    lo = gt + 1;
  } else {
    threeWayQuicksort(a, gt + 1, hi);
    hi = lt - 1;
  }

  if (lo < hi) threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, hi);
}
```

## Test Plan
- **All equal**: Verify no swaps and linear time
- **Already sorted**: Confirm correct partitioning and maintain sorted order
- **No duplicates**: Validate against standard quicksort behavior
- **Random with duplicates**: Compare against built-in sort for correctness
- **Large duplicate ratio**: Measure performance improvement vs standard quicksort

## Complexity Comparison
- **Three-way quicksort**: O(n log m) average, O(n²) worst, O(log n) space
- **Standard mergesort**: O(n log n) always, O(n) extra space

Optimal when m ≪ n (many duplicate keys).

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes understanding of sorting trade-offs (in-place memory vs stability vs constants) and how hybrid approaches balance speed, especially relevant in finance and large-scale data.

## Key Concepts

- 3-way partitioning in quicksort
- Insertion sort for small slices
- In-place sorting and O(1) extra space
- Average vs worst-case complexities; pivot choice

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortHybrid(a){
  const TH = 16;
  const swap=(i,j)=>{ [a[i],a[j]]=[a[j],a[i]]; };
  const insertSort=(l,r)=>{
    for(let i=l+1;i<=r;i++){
      let x=a[i], j=i-1;
      while(j>=l && a[j]>x){ a[j+1]=a[j]; j--; }
      a[j+1]=x;
    }
  };
  const partition=(lo,hi)=>{
    const p=a[lo+((hi-lo)>>1)];
    let i=lo, lt=lo, gt=hi;
    while(i<=gt){
      if(a[i]<p){ swap(i,lt); i++; lt++; }
      else if(a[i]>p){ swap(i,gt); gt--; }
      else i++;
    }
    return [lt,gt];
  };
  const qs=(lo,hi)=>{
    if(lo>=hi) return;
    if(hi-lo+1 <= TH){ insertSort(lo,hi); return; }
    const [l,r] = partition(lo,hi);
    qs(lo, l-1);
    qs(r+1, hi);
  };
  qs(0,a.length-1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Duplicates are common in real data and inputs are often skewed. A 3-way partition quicksort directly handles the middle bucket of equal keys, avoiding repeated swaps of equal elements and preventing degeneracy.

## Key Concepts

- 3-way partitioning (Dutch national flag style) for handling duplicates
- Pivot selection strategies (median-of-three to reduce worst-case risk)
- Introsort/hybrid switching to guarantee worst-case bounds
- In-place sorting with O(log n) recursion depth

## Code Example

```javascript
function threeWayQuicksort(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  while (lo < hi) {
    let lt = lo, i = lo + 1, gt = hi;
    const pivot = a[lo];
    while (i <= gt) {
      if (a[i] < pivot) { [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
      else if (a[i] > pivot) { [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]]; gt--; }
      else { i++; }
    }
    threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, lt - 1);
    lo = gt; // tail recursion on the right side
  }
  return a;
}
```

